<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acemhöyük</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Acemhöyük is a large settlement mound near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksaray">Aksaray</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, occupied from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a>. Excavations since 1962 have uncovered two palaces of the 19th and 18th centuries BCE, with clay sealings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivories</a> and goods linked to the Old Assyrian trade network. Many scholars identify it with Purushanda (Burushattum), a wealthy city named in the Assyrian merchants' letters, though the identification is debated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acemhoyuk.jpg" alt="Acemhöyük" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Near_East_topographic_map-blank.svg: Sémhur derivative work: Al-qamar · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KarumKanis.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
